PR #35  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Undisclosed implementation-defined (G, psm, rnd) floating-point grouping and rounding parameters in matrix multiply-accumulate instructions cause silent bit-level numerical divergence across implementations, breaking portable numerical reproducibility guarantees that software may rely on.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:458-458  (header)
      CSR_VTYPE #define 0xc21 is the architectural CSR number for the vector extension's vtype register. It encodes SEW, VLMUL, VTA, VMA, VILL — all V-extension semantics. No matrix-specific CSR (e.g., for G/psm/rnd) is defined anywhere in this header. The definition itself is architecturally correct.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:461-476  (header)
      VTYPE_VLMUL, VTYPE_VSEW, VTYPE_VTA, VTYPE_VMA, VTYPE_VILL bitfields correctly encode V-extension control fields per the RISC-V V spec. These control register grouping (LMUL) and element width (SEW) for vectors, not matrix-specific G/psm/rnd parameters. The T-Head variant bitfields (VTYPE_VLMUL_THEAD, VTYPE_VSEW_THEAD, VTYPE_VEDIV_THEAD) are correctly separated with different bit positions.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/vector.h:129-165  __vstate_csr_save
      Saves CSR_VTYPE, CSR_VSTART, CSR_VL, and CSR_VCSR — all V-extension CSRs. No matrix-specific state is saved because the kernel has no awareness of any matrix extension. If the implementation has matrix architectural state (e.g., matrix accumulator registers, MCFG CSR), it is NOT saved/restored across context switches. This is an implicit invariant: the kernel assumes the matrix extension doesn't exist or has no architected state needing context management. The vcsr handling for T-Head (lines 154-159) correctly manages FS bit for vxrm/vxsat access but this is V-extension fixed-point rounding, not matrix psm/rnd.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/vector.h:167-194  __vstate_csr_restore
      Restores vtype via 'vsetvl x0, %2, %1' — this sets SEW/VLMUL grouping for the V extension. If a matrix extension reuses or extends vtype semantics for G/psm/rnd control (e.g., VLMUL encoding also implies matrix grouping factor G), then a carefully crafted vtype value flowing through ptrace or sigreturn could silently alter matrix computation behaviour. The vsetvl instruction uses the source vtype directly with no additional sanitisation here — validation is pushed entirely to callers (invalid_ptrace_v_csr / signal frame construction). The T-Head path (lines 177-193) also restores vxrm/vxsat from vcsr, which is V-extension fixed-point rounding, not matrix psm/rnd.
